    "millsfield:images_texts": [
      "NC 88884\nwAy\nLEOPOLDVILLED\nPan American inaugurates commercial landplane service\ninto Belgian _\nCongo, January 19, 1946\nDouglas Db-4",
      "Historic First Flights of Pan American Clippers\nPan American inaugurates commercial landplane service into\nBelgian Congo,\nJanuary 19, 1946\nThe arrival of Pan Am's Douglas DC-4 (NC88884) in Leopoldville marks the first scheduled United States-\nflag transatlantic passenger flight into the Belgian Congo with landplanes. Commanded by Captain Audrey Durst,\nthe flight inaugurated bi-monthly service between New York and Leopoldville via Newfoundland, Lisbon, and\nThe DC-4, which first saw service during World War Il as a military transport, marked the transition to\nlandplanes by airlines flying overseas. The introduction of the commercial DC-4 was the beginning of the end for\nPan American's world-famous flying boats.\nAbout the Artist\nIn 1926, John T. McCoy became a working student for the Curtiss Flying Ser-\nvice, Curtiss Field, Long Island, New York.\nWhen Charles A. Lindbergh arrived at Curtiss Field, his SPIRIT OF ST. LOUIS\nwas housed in one of the Curtiss hangers. It was at this time that McCoy met\nLindbergh, who was most cordial and friendly to those who worked on the flight\nline. In those days muscle power was the method used for starting engines, ac-\ncomplished by swinging the propeller by hand. McCoy often pulled a prop through\nfor Lindbergh.\nYears later, when McCoy was commissioned to do paintings depicting the\nhistory of Pan American World Airways, Inc. - entitled HISTORIC FIRST FLIGHTS\nOF PAN AMERICAN CLIPPERS - he consulted with Lindbergh on the selection of\naircraft and locations for the series.\nThis is one picture in a series of thirteen. If you would like to purchase 8½\"x 11\"\nor 16\" x 20\" limited edition prints, send your letter of inquiry to: Public Relations\nDepartment - Pan American World Airways, Inc., Pan Am Building, New York, New\nYork 10166. Please include code number 304 in your letter.",
      "Noilly Prat Vermouth\nManhattan\nBloody Mary\nJ. Walker Black\nCanadian Club\nAperitifs\nDry Sack\nHarvey Bristol Cream\nCampari\nCocktails\nVodka Martini\nGin Martini\nWhiskey Sour\nScrewdriver\nDaiquiri\nTom Collins\nVodka Collins\nSpirits\nJ\u0026 B Scotch\nSmirnoff Vodka\nBeefeater Gin\nOld Grand Dad\nJack Daniels Bacardi\nVines\nMâcon Villages La Forêt\nLes Clés du Roi\nChampagne\nCharbaut\nLiqueurs \u0026 Cognac\nBénédictine D.O.M.\nDrambuie\nKahlúa\nGrand Marnier\nAmaretto di Saronno\nCognac Camus Napoléon\nRIzP\nBeverages\nCoca-Cola\nTab\nCanada Dry\nAssorted Beers\nFruit Juices",
      "noV\nTUOC\nanilloD solbo\nybooid\nfusdis!\n3b s\nans8\nyIG sbens?\n500L",
      "Menu\nPomme Farcie\nStuffed Baked Potato\nCaviar Garni\nFresh Caviar served with traditional accompaniments of hard cooked egg,\nonion, sour cream, toast triangles and blinis\nHors d'Oeuvre Variés\nSelected Hors d'Oeuvres\nSalade Verte Mêlée\nFresh garden salad presented from the Dining Service Cart\nRôti au Vol\nSirloin of Beef roasted on board and carved to order. Served with an assortment of sauces\nPoulet Wellington\nTender breast of chicken baked in puff pastry\nHomard Thermidor\nLobster chunks in a rich brandy cream sauce\nBouquetière de Légumes\nau Beurre\nButtered Vegetable Bouquet\nRiz Pilaf aux\nAmandes et Raisins\nYellow Rice with Raisins and Almonds\nFruits et Fromages\nFresh Fruit and Selected Cheeses\nSouflé Grand Marnier\nGrand Marnier Souffle\nCoffe\nor\nCafé Décaféiné\nSanka\nTarte au Fromage\nCheesecake\nThé\nTea\nPlease accept our apology if, due to previous passenger selections, your choice is not available.\n257K\n12-15-82"
